CHECK name=rt-ids-are-a-selection verdict=PASS the honest record's 8 ids are in range, distinct, and the two tie rules selected the same 8 experts in some order (this row has no exact tie at the boundary, so the rules coincide here — `topk-selection-perturbations-redden-ids` is what shows they need not)
CHECK name=rt-poison-census-zero-on-the-honest-record verdict=PASS all 8 fields of the honest router record are fully written: ids=0,ids_alt=0,logits=0,logits_f32=0,probs=0,wcast=0,wleak=0,wpre=0. The non-zero leg is controlled separately, and a census that could only read 0 would prove nothing either way
CHECK name=rt-ulp-ruler-matches-the-licensor verdict=PASS `wpre` measured twice, once by `engine/block_gate.py`::ulp_diff (the licensor's ruler, aggregate only) and once per slot by `rt_ulp_slots` here: licensor ndiff=5 max_ulp=2, here ndiff=5 max_ulp=2 over 8 slots. The two folds are the same map widened to int64, and if they ever disagree the ULP number in this file means something different from the ULP number in every band
CHECK name=rt-wpre-scope-derived-from-artifacts verdict=PASS the scope on `wpre` (9 ULP on pair `rt_hip_device_vs_rt_hip_hostpath`) is the strictest of three priors — a licensor floor probe, §R5's wording, and an error propagation from §mr_exp's — and none of them is this build's output. The dependence on the licensor's artifact is witnessed, not claimed: the triangle prior is exactly twice `weights_vs_pinned_f32_ulp` as the artifact reads it (5 x 2 = 10), and re-deriving with that number set to 1 in memory makes the prior 2 (the binding limit would then be 2). A bound typed into this file would not move. Wording the driver cannot find in its source documents is a refusal, not a default
CHECK name=rt-wpre-premise-measured-against-the-pin verdict=PASS the triangle-inequality prior only holds if both compilations are themselves inside the licensed band of the pinned pre-cast weights: device=4,host_twin=2 ULP against a licensed `weights_vs_pinned_f32_ulp` of 5. If a route drifts past its own licence the premise fails and this reddens, rather than letting a bound derived from two licensed legs license a third number nobody licensed
CHECK name=rt-device-launch-counted verdict=PASS the device arm counted at least one launch and one HIP call; the flag is not the evidence, the counter is
CHECK name=rt-device-host-licensed-fields-identical verdict=PASS device arm vs host twin, zero tolerance on the fields a licence measures at exactness: ids=0/8,ids_alt=0/8,wcast=0/8 (whole record ndiff_bytes=237 of 3232, which is not this claim — the rest of those bytes are the `wpre` scope and the long-dot `probs`, both printed and neither absorbed). The tolerance-free form is the point: one differing bit of `topk_ids` or of the bf16 `topk_weights` reddens this check and cannot hide inside `wpre`'s measured spread, because those rows are licensed at exactness by `speccheck/spec.md §10.6` and the licensor's floor probe measured 164 f32 ULP of margin against a 5 ULP route spread before it agreed the cast was robust
CHECK name=rt-device-host-wpre-within-derived-scope verdict=PASS `wpre` (the fp32 pre-cast renormalised weight) between the two compilations: 5 of 8 slots differ, worst 2 ULP, per-slot 1,1,1,0,0,0,2,1, against the derived scope of 9 ULP in `WPRESCOPE`. This is a re-derivation, not a relaxation: §R5 documents the field as not bit-exact across an independent fp32 route, the limit comes from that document, §mr_exp's, and the licensor's floor probe, and `CONTROL name=wpre-scope-rejects-a-bigger-perturbation` witnesses that the limit rejects a larger-than-scope perturbation. Measuring the spread and enforcing what came out would be a gate that reddens on nothing
CHECK name=rt-device-host-difference-is-the-documented-exp-scope verdict=PASS attribution, because 'the two libms differ' is a story until each named condition is separately answerable: R1's outputs bit-identical=True, `wcast` bit-identical=True, every moving `wpre` slot has its own `probs` addend moving=True, fields moving outside the exp() path=none. All four hold, so the difference is the documented `exp()` scope and the fix belongs in the check. If any had failed it would be a codegen defect and the fix would belong in `engine/device/m2_rt_kernels.h`, which is why this is a check and not a sentence in a log
CHECK name=control-legs-cover-the-path-under-claim verdict=PASS 2 of 10 controls declare which path they covered, and every declared path has at least one arm act counted off that arm's own `RUN path=` report: topk-selection-perturbations-redden-ids=host_cpubody:2,device:2; wpre-scope-rejects-a-bigger-perturbation=host_cpubody:1,device:1. Controls declaring no path (their legs are plan/refusal legs, not path legs): device-claim-scan-is-awake,launch-failure-exits-red,poison-census-can-be-nonzero,resident-span-corrupt,router-dtype-order-distinguishable-in-arm,router-poison-census-can-be-nonzero,router-span-is-f32,wrong-modes-further-from-pins. Window e2 is the reason this check exists: `topk-selection-perturbations-redden-ids` claimed the device path while both its legs ran on the host twin, and read SILENT in the mode everything above M2 is built on
CHECK name=device-path-claims-executed-on-device verdict=PASS a device run has to have run the perturbation controls on the device, not merely beside it: 3 control legs executed under `path=device` (the licensed-row controls need their tie and bound legs on the arm they license). In a CPU run this check is vacuous by construction and says so, because there is no device path to claim
